#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
ffrife - standalone rife-ncnn-vulkan (AI frame interpolation) wrapper.

Not JW/Bible-specific: takes any local file or remote URL, an optional
time window and ffmpeg -vf filter chain, and produces a real-AI-interpolated
(not motion-blended) output at a target frame rate in a single lossy
re-encode generation. `slverse` uses this as a library (see its import of
ffrife via importlib) instead of carrying its own copy of this logic;
other tools (e.g. `ffslow`'s --rife flag) can shell out to this CLI directly.

  ffrife setup                          interactive: download/configure the
                                         rife-ncnn-vulkan binary
  ffrife <input> [-o <output>] [opts]   interpolate (default output:
                                         <input>_rife<ext> alongside it)

Options:
  --window A-B           trim to A..B (clock times may contain colons)
  --start T --end T       absolute source endpoints; omit both for whole input
  --fps N                target frame rate: a plain number (60) or
                          source-relative (2x, 2.5x, 150%) (default 60)
  --speed N               retime the output (0.5 = half-speed slow-motion,
                          3 = 3x fast-forward), folded into the same merge/
                          fallback encode via setpts+atempo - no extra pass
  --vf FILTER             extra ffmpeg -vf filter chain, applied in the SAME
                          pass that extracts frames (no extra re-encode)
  --fallback E            none|framerate|minterpolate - what to do if RIFE
                          isn't installed/configured (default: none)
  --container mp4|mkv     output container - overrides -o/the source's own
                          extension

On a plain untrimmed, unretimed run, the ONLY thing
that changes is the frame rate: subtitles, chapters, attachments (fonts,
etc.), extra audio tracks, embedded cover art, and global container tags all
carry over from the source by default, same as a plain ffmpeg passthrough
would. Each has its own --no-<thing>/keep_<thing> config flag to drop it
instead (--no-subtitles, --no-chapters, --no-extra-audio-tracks,
--no-attachments, --no-thumbnail, --no-source-metadata); attachments are
always dropped for an mp4/mov-family container regardless, since that
container format has no equivalent. --start/--end clip and rebase the
time-sensitive streams; --speed drops them because arbitrary subtitle
formats cannot be retimed losslessly. Attachments/thumbnail/source-metadata
have no time dimension and remain eligible.
"""
